# T6.2: Implement SHA-256 Checksum Comparison and Inline Diff

## What to build
Compare current command output against stored golden files using SHA-256 checksums,
and show inline diffs when mismatches are detected.

## Rust implementation
```rust
use std::collections::BTreeMap;

/// Golden file entry
struct GoldenEntry {
    command: Vec<String>,
    checksum: String,       // SHA-256 hex
    normalized_output: String,
}

/// Golden manifest (stored as JSON)
struct GoldenManifest {
    version: u32,
    entries: BTreeMap<String, GoldenEntry>,  // name → entry
}

fn compute_sha256(content: &str) -> String {
    // Use sha2 crate or implement basic SHA-256
    // The project already uses crypto for share/export, check existing deps
}

fn compare_golden(
    name: &str,
    current: &str,
    expected: &GoldenEntry,
) -> GoldenComparison {
    let current_checksum = compute_sha256(current);
    if current_checksum == expected.checksum {
        GoldenComparison::Match
    } else {
        // Generate inline diff
        let diff = generate_diff(&expected.normalized_output, current);
        GoldenComparison::Mismatch { diff, expected_checksum, actual_checksum }
    }
}

fn generate_diff(expected: &str, actual: &str) -> String {
    // Simple line-by-line diff with +/- prefixes
    // No need for a full diff algorithm — just show lines that differ
}
```

## Implementation notes
- Check if sha2 or ring is already in workspace dependencies (share crate uses crypto)
- If not available, can compute SHA-256 via std::process::Command("sha256sum") as fallback,
  but prefer a Rust implementation for cross-platform
- The diff output should be human-readable (similar to git diff --no-index)
- For small outputs (<100 lines), show full diff. For larger outputs, show first 20
  differing lines with a "... and N more differences" summary.

