# Track 8: Engine Integration

## What
Wire all ATC subsystems into a single AtcEngine that runs on the poller tick
and integrates with the existing server infrastructure. This is the glue that
makes the ATC operational.

## AtcEngine Struct
```rust
pub struct AtcEngine {
    /// Whether the ATC is enabled (AM_ATC_ENABLED env var)
    enabled: bool,
    /// Whether the ATC has registered itself as an agent
    registered: bool,
    /// Project key(s) the ATC is managing (discovered from DB stats)
    tracked_projects: HashSet<String>,
    /// Decision cores (one per subsystem)
    liveness_core: DecisionCore<LivenessState, LivenessAction>,
    conflict_core: DecisionCore<ConflictState, ConflictAction>,
    routing_core: DecisionCore<LoadState, LoadAction>,
    /// Subsystem state
    liveness: LivenessDetector,
    conflict: ConflictDetector,
    router: LoadRouter,
    synthesizer: SessionSynthesizer,
    /// Cross-cutting
    calibration: CalibrationGuard,
    martingale: MartingaleRegretEngine, // Track 11
    predictive: PredictiveIntelligence, // Track 12
    ledger: EvidenceLedger,
    /// Incremental computation (Adapton-style generation tracking)
    last_processed_event_seq: u64,
    last_db_stats_gen: u64,
    generation: u64,
    /// The ATC's own agent name (for self-exclusion filtering)
    self_agent_name: &'static str, // "AirTrafficControl"
}
```

## Self-Exclusion (Critical)
The ATC reads ALL events including its own messages. Without self-exclusion:
- The ATC's health probes would count as "activity" for the probed agent
- The ATC's advisory messages would create phantom conflict edges
- The ATC would track its own liveness (always alive, pointless)

Solution: every event processing path filters by agent name:
```rust
const ATC_AGENT_NAME: &str = "AirTrafficControl";

fn is_atc_event(event: &MailEvent) -> bool {
    event.from_agent().is_some_and(|a| a == ATC_AGENT_NAME)
        || event.to_agents().iter().any(|a| a == ATC_AGENT_NAME)
}
```

Only count events FROM or TO real agents, never ATC-originated events.
Exception: probe RESPONSES from real agents DO count as liveness evidence.

## Tick Integration
The ATC runs on the same thread as the poller, after each DB snapshot update:

```rust
impl AtcEngine {
    /// Called by the poller loop after each snapshot update.
    /// Budget: must complete within 5ms to avoid blocking the poller.
    fn tick(&mut self, state: &TuiSharedState, db_snapshot: &DbStatSnapshot) {
        if !self.enabled { return; }

        let tick_start = Instant::now();

        // 0. Self-registration (first tick after DB is ready)
        if !self.registered && db_snapshot.agents > 0 {
            self.register_self(state, db_snapshot);
        }

        // 1. Discover new projects (for multi-project scoping)
        for project in &db_snapshot.projects_list {
            self.tracked_projects.insert(project.slug.clone());
        }

        // 2. Process new events since last tick (incremental)
        let new_events = state.events_since_limited(
            self.last_processed_event_seq, 256
        );
        for event in &new_events {
            if is_atc_event(event) && !is_probe_response(event) {
                continue; // self-exclusion
            }
            self.liveness.process_event(event);
            self.conflict.process_event(event, &self.tracked_projects);
            self.router.process_event(event);
            self.synthesizer.absorb_event(event);
        }
        if let Some(last) = new_events.last() {
            self.last_processed_event_seq = last.seq();
        }

        // 3. Run liveness checks (only for agents with stale signals)
        let liveness_actions = self.liveness.evaluate(
            &self.liveness_core, &self.calibration
        );
        for (agent, action) in liveness_actions {
            self.execute_liveness_action(state, &agent, action);
        }

        // 4. Run conflict detection (only if reservations changed)
        let db_gen = state.db_stats_generation();
        if db_gen != self.last_db_stats_gen {
            self.last_db_stats_gen = db_gen;
            let conflict_actions = self.conflict.evaluate(
                &self.conflict_core, &self.calibration, db_snapshot
            );
            for action in conflict_actions {
                self.execute_conflict_action(state, action);
            }
        }

        // 5. Update martingale engine (e-process + CUSUM + regret)
        self.martingale.update(&self.ledger);

        // 6. Update calibration guard (consumes martingale signals)
        self.calibration.update_from_martingale(&self.martingale);

        // 7. Run predictive intelligence (thread participation + probing)
        self.predictive.tick(
            &new_events, &self.liveness, &self.conflict, &self.ledger
        );

        // 8. Budget check
        let elapsed = tick_start.elapsed();
        if elapsed > Duration::from_millis(5) {
            tracing::warn!(
                elapsed_ms = elapsed.as_millis(),
                "ATC tick exceeded 5ms budget"
            );
        }

        self.generation += 1;
    }
}
```

## MCP Self-Registration
On first tick after DB is ready, the ATC registers itself using the server's
own tool dispatch. The project_key is discovered from the first project in
db_snapshot.projects_list:

```rust
fn register_self(&mut self, state: &TuiSharedState, snapshot: &DbStatSnapshot) {
    if let Some(project) = snapshot.projects_list.first() {
        // Use direct sync DB writes (same mechanism as atc_send_message below)
        // INSERT OR IGNORE into agents table
        // The ATC is now visible in the agents list and can be messaged
        let project_key = &project.slug;
        // register_agent(project_key, "AirTrafficControl",
        //                "mcp-agent-mail", "atc-rules-engine")
        self.registered = true;
    }
}
```

## Sync Message-Sending Mechanism (Critical)
The ATC runs on the sync poller thread (std::thread), but the MCP tool
dispatch runs on the async server. To bridge this gap, the ATC uses DIRECT
SYNC DB WRITES — the same pattern the CLI crate uses (see MEMORY.md:
"CLI Uses Sync SQLite" — open_db_sync() helper with sqlmodel_sqlite).

```rust
/// Send a message from the ATC agent directly via sync SQLite.
fn atc_send_message(
    conn: &DbConn,
    project_id: i64,
    to_agent: &str,
    subject: &str,
    body: &str,
    thread_id: &str,
) -> Result<i64, McpError> {
    // INSERT INTO messages (from_agent, to_agent, subject, body_md, ...)
    // The git archive coalescer picks up new messages automatically.
    // Returns the new message ID.
}
```

This bypasses MCP validation (no JSON-RPC overhead) but must enforce the
same constraints manually (agent registered, project exists, valid names).
The evidence ledger captures these direct writes for auditability.

The ATC opens ONE sync DB connection at startup (in PollerConnectionState)
and reuses it — no per-message connection overhead.

For self-registration, also use direct sync DB writes:
```rust
fn atc_register_self(conn: &DbConn, project_id: i64) -> Result<(), McpError> {
    // INSERT OR IGNORE INTO agents (name, project_id, program, model, ...)
}
```

## Configuration (Environment Variables)
| Variable | Default | Description |
|----------|---------|-------------|
| AM_ATC_ENABLED | true | Master switch |
| AM_ATC_PROBE_INTERVAL_SECS | 120 | Health probe frequency |
| AM_ATC_ADVISORY_COOLDOWN_SECS | 300 | Min interval between advisories to same agent |
| AM_ATC_SUMMARY_INTERVAL_SECS | 300 | Session summary posting interval |
| AM_ATC_SAFE_MODE_COVERAGE | 0.85 | Calibration guard threshold |
| AM_ATC_FALSE_POSITIVE_BUDGET | 0.01 | Max false death rate per agent-hour |
| AM_ATC_LEDGER_CAPACITY | 1000 | Evidence ring buffer size |
| AM_ATC_DEADLOCK_GRACE_SECS | 30 | Time before auto-releasing deadlocked reservations |
| AM_ATC_TICK_BUDGET_MS | 5 | Max time per tick before warning |

## CPU Budget Enforcement
The ATC tick has a hard budget. The incremental computation pattern ensures
idle ticks cost ~0 (no new events → skip all processing). The Adapton-style
generation counter (same pattern as TuiThemePalette cache from this session's
earlier work) gates expensive recomputation.

## Files
- `crates/mcp-agent-mail-server/src/atc.rs` (main engine, new file)
- `crates/mcp-agent-mail-core/src/config.rs` (new AM_ATC_* env vars)
- `crates/mcp-agent-mail-server/src/tui_poller.rs` (tick call site)

## Dependency
- Requires Track 1 (Decision Core) — instantiates 3 decision cores
- Requires Track 2 (Liveness) — wires liveness detector
- Requires Track 3 (Conflict) — wires conflict detector
- Requires Track 4 (Evidence Ledger) — wires audit trail
- Requires Track 5 (Calibration) — wires safety guard
- Tracks 6 (Load Routing) and 7 (Synthesis) wired in later (P1)
- Track 11 (Martingale Regret Engine) wired into calibration pipeline
- Track 12 (Predictive Intelligence) wired into tick loop

## Tests
- Unit: disabled ATC does zero work on tick
- Unit: first tick after DB ready registers AirTrafficControl agent
- Unit: ATC events filtered via self-exclusion
- Unit: probe responses NOT filtered (they're evidence)
- Unit: tick processes only new events (incremental, checks seq)
- Unit: tick budget enforcement (>5ms → warning in tracing)
- Unit: new projects discovered from db_snapshot
- Integration: ATC sends messages via MCP tools
- Integration: ATC decisions appear in evidence ledger
- E2E: full ATC lifecycle (register, detect stale, resolve conflict, summarize)

# D.2: Wire BayesianDiffStrategy into TUI render loop

## Why this debt exists

Original `br-v3hid` was closed in pass 2 with the `BayesianDiffStrategy` module shipped (D.1, present in `crates/mcp-agent-mail-server/src/tui_decision.rs` — 1090 LOC + 39 tests) but the actual WIRING this bead exists for is absent. The module is consumed only by `benches/frame_bench.rs` and `tests/alien_integration.rs`. There is no integration into `tui_app.rs`'s render loop.

## Spec — preserves all original requirements verbatim

The `BayesianDiffStrategy` from D.1 must be integrated into the actual TUI render loop in the server crate. The current render path in the FrankenTUI integration uses `ftui-runtime` for frame scheduling and `ftui::Frame` for buffer management.

### Adoption wedge (verbatim)

1. Add a `BayesianDiffStrategy` instance to the TUI application state struct (`MailApp` in `tui_app.rs`).
2. At the start of each frame render:
   a. Compute `FrameState` from the current frame metrics (change count from buffer diff, resize flag from terminal events, budget from `AnimationBudget`).
   b. Call `strategy.observe(&frame_state)` to get the `DiffAction`.
   c. Based on the action:
      - `Incremental`: render only dirty regions (use ftui's differential rendering).
      - `Full`: force full redraw (`frame.force_redraw()`).
      - `Deferred`: skip rendering, schedule next frame sooner.
3. Record the decision to the evidence ledger with the FrameState as evidence.
4. After rendering, backfill the outcome: was the frame completed within budget? Did the incremental diff miss any changes (detected by occasional full-diff audit frames)?

### Risks / Safe Mode (verbatim)

- Risk: incorrect integration causes visual glitches. Mitigation: full diff is always correct; only risk is choosing incremental when full is needed.
- Fallback trigger: if visual glitch reports exceed 0 in testing, switch to deterministic_fallback.
- Safe mode: start with `deterministic_fallback = true`, progressively enable Bayesian.

### Validation (verbatim)

- Render 1000 frames with synthetic data mutations. Verify:
  1. No visual glitches (compare Bayesian render output to full-diff render output).
  2. Frame budget adherence ≥ 95% of frames complete within 16ms.
  3. Bayesian mode skips fewer frames than naive "always full diff" strategy.

## Current state

- D.1 module: `crates/mcp-agent-mail-server/src/tui_decision.rs` exports:
  - `BayesianDiffStrategy { observe, deterministic_fallback }`
  - `FrameState { change_count, resize_flag, budget_ms }`
  - `DiffAction::{ Incremental, Full, Deferred }`
- D.1 tests: 39 unit tests in `tui_decision.rs::tests`.
- D.1 bench: `benches/frame_bench.rs` (consumed by D.1 verification only).
- TUI application state: `MailApp` struct in `crates/mcp-agent-mail-server/src/tui_app.rs`.
- Render loop: `MailApp::render_frame()` (or similar) wraps `ftui::Frame`.
- Evidence ledger: `crates/mcp-agent-mail-core/src/evidence_ledger.rs` with `append()`.
- AnimationBudget: lives in TUI animation module (verify path; likely `crates/mcp-agent-mail-server/src/tui_animation.rs` or in `frankentui`).

## Target state — wedge wiring + audit hook + safe-mode rollout

### Step 1 — `MailApp` gets `diff_strategy: BayesianDiffStrategy` field

```rust
pub struct MailApp {
    // ... existing fields ...
    diff_strategy: BayesianDiffStrategy,
    diff_strategy_safe_mode: bool, // true: deterministic_fallback only; default for first ship
    full_diff_audit_every: u32,    // every Nth frame, do a full diff and compare to incremental
    full_diff_audit_counter: u32,
}
```

### Step 2 — Per-frame integration in render loop

```rust
fn render_frame(&mut self, frame_state: FrameState) -> RenderOutcome {
    let action = if self.diff_strategy_safe_mode {
        // Safe-mode shadow: ALWAYS render full, but ALSO observe + log so we can A/B compare
        let _ = self.diff_strategy.observe(&frame_state);
        DiffAction::Full
    } else {
        self.diff_strategy.observe(&frame_state)
    };
    self.evidence_ledger.append(EvidenceKind::FrameDecision { state: frame_state.clone(), action });

    let outcome = match action {
        DiffAction::Incremental => self.render_incremental(),
        DiffAction::Full        => self.render_full(),
        DiffAction::Deferred    => self.skip_frame_and_reschedule(),
    };

    // Periodic audit: every Nth frame, redo as full diff and compare to detect missed changes.
    self.full_diff_audit_counter = self.full_diff_audit_counter.wrapping_add(1);
    if self.full_diff_audit_counter % self.full_diff_audit_every == 0 && action == DiffAction::Incremental {
        let audit = self.render_full_for_audit();
        if audit.differs_from_incremental() {
            self.evidence_ledger.append(EvidenceKind::DiffAuditMismatch { frame_state, action, audit_diff: audit.summary });
            // Auto-flip into safe mode if mismatches accumulate
            self.consecutive_audit_mismatches += 1;
            if self.consecutive_audit_mismatches >= 3 {
                self.diff_strategy_safe_mode = true;
                tracing::warn!(target = "mcp_agent_mail::tui::diff", "auto-flipped into safe_mode after 3 audit mismatches");
            }
        } else {
            self.consecutive_audit_mismatches = 0;
        }
    }

    self.evidence_ledger.backfill_outcome(action, outcome);
    outcome
}
```

### Step 3 — `FrameState` computation from real metrics

`compute_frame_state(&self, terminal_events: &[Event], render_budget_ms: u32) -> FrameState`:
- `change_count`: count dirty regions in buffer-diff (already computed by ftui)
- `resize_flag`: any `Resize` event in `terminal_events`
- `budget_ms`: from `AnimationBudget::current_remaining()`

### Step 4 — Diagnostics (cross-references E6 / br-j1qwr)

Tracing target `mcp_agent_mail::tui::diff`. Events:
- `frame_decision_start` (DEBUG) per frame: { frame_state, action, safe_mode }
- `frame_completed` (DEBUG): { action, duration_ms, within_budget }
- `frame_audit_mismatch` (WARN): { frame_state, action, summary } — never silently swallow
- `diff_strategy_safe_mode_flipped` (WARN): when auto-flip fires

### Step 5 — Env knobs

- `AM_TUI_DIFF_SAFE_MODE` (default `true` initially; flip to `false` after soak-bake) — start in safe mode
- `AM_TUI_FULL_DIFF_AUDIT_EVERY` (default `64`) — audit cadence
- `AM_TUI_DIFF_AUDIT_AUTO_FLIP_THRESHOLD` (default `3`) — auto-flip mismatch count

## Non-goals

- No changes to `BayesianDiffStrategy` math (D.1 done).
- Not implementing a new differential renderer — uses ftui's existing differential rendering primitive.
- Not deprecating the previous render path — safe mode keeps full-redraw available.

# Track 11: Conformal Martingale Regret Engine

## Graveyard Sources
- §0.18 Sequential Testing Recipes (Anytime-Valid)
- §0.19 Evidence Ledger Schema (Machine-Readable)
- §12.1 Conformal Prediction [Tier A]
- §12.13 Online Change-Point Detection (ADWIN/CUSUM/Page-Hinkley) [Tier A]
- §12.3 Online Convex Optimization / Regret Minimization [Tier A]

## What This Changes
This track REPLACES the simple coverage-counting approach in Track 5 with
a mathematically rigorous, provably correct monitoring system that combines
three breakthrough ideas from the alien graveyard into one coherent engine:

1. **Conformal e-process martingale** for anytime-valid miscalibration detection
2. **CUSUM change-point detector** for regime-shift awareness
3. **Online regret tracking with counterfactual analysis** for self-improvement

The result: the ATC doesn't just detect "am I accurate enough?" — it detects
"has the world changed?", "which decisions was I wrong about?", "what should
I have done differently?", and "is my error pattern getting worse or better?"

## Why This Is Radical
Traditional monitoring checks "is accuracy above 85%?" That's:
- **Batch-oriented**: valid only after N samples, not at arbitrary stopping times
- **Insensitive to patterns**: 85% accuracy with all errors on the same agent
  is MUCH worse than 85% accuracy spread uniformly
- **Backward-looking**: tells you what happened, not what's changing

The conformal martingale approach is:
- **Anytime-valid**: check at any moment, no multiple-testing penalty
- **Pattern-aware**: detects WHICH subsystem/agent is drifting, not just overall
- **Forward-looking**: detects regime changes before they cause widespread harm
- **Self-improving**: counterfactual regret analysis identifies systematic biases

## Core Component 1: E-Process Martingale Monitor

### Theory
An e-process is a non-negative supermartingale starting at E₀ = 1 under the
null hypothesis. If E_t >= 1/α at any time t, we have level-α evidence against
H₀. This is ALWAYS valid — you can check after every decision, stop whenever
you want, and the false positive guarantee still holds.

For the ATC, H₀ = "the ATC's predictions are well-calibrated (coverage >= target)".

### Implementation
```rust
struct EProcessMonitor {
    /// Running e-process value (starts at 1.0)
    e_value: f64,
    /// Target coverage rate
    target_coverage: f64,
    /// Alert threshold (default: 20.0 = 5% significance level)
    alert_threshold: f64,
    /// Per-subsystem e-processes (detect which subsystem is drifting)
    per_subsystem: HashMap<AtcSubsystem, f64>,
    /// Per-agent e-processes (detect which agent is causing issues)
    per_agent: HashMap<String, f64>,
}

impl EProcessMonitor {
    /// Update after observing a prediction outcome.
    ///
    /// The betting strategy uses the GROW (Gambling Researcher's Online Wealth)
    /// martingale: E_{t+1} = E_t * (1 + λ_t * (Z_t - α))
    /// where Z_t = 1 if prediction was wrong, 0 if correct,
    /// α is the target miscalibration rate, and λ_t is the bet size.
    fn update(&mut self, outcome: &PredictionOutcome) {
        let z = if outcome.correct { 0.0 } else { 1.0 };
        let alpha = 1.0 - self.target_coverage;

        // Adaptive bet size: bet more when we have more evidence of drift
        // λ ∈ [-1/α, 1/(1-α)] to keep the martingale non-negative
        let lambda = self.adaptive_bet_size(z, alpha);

        // Global e-process update
        self.e_value *= 1.0 + lambda * (z - alpha);

        // Per-subsystem update
        let sub_e = self.per_subsystem
            .entry(outcome.subsystem)
            .or_insert(1.0);
        *sub_e *= 1.0 + lambda * (z - alpha);

        // Per-agent update
        if let Some(agent) = &outcome.agent {
            let agent_e = self.per_agent
                .entry(agent.clone())
                .or_insert(1.0);
            *agent_e *= 1.0 + lambda * (z - alpha);
        }
    }

    /// Adaptive bet sizing using the mixture method:
    /// average over a grid of λ values to avoid committing to one bet size.
    fn adaptive_bet_size(&self, z: f64, alpha: f64) -> f64 {
        // ONS (Online Newton Step) adaptive bet — nearly optimal.
        // Achieves O(log T) wealth growth vs. O(√T) for fixed λ.
        //
        // Maintains running sufficient statistics (stored in self):
        //   sum_centered: Σ (z_i - α)
        //   sum_sq:       Σ (z_i - α)²
        //   count:        number of observations
        //
        // λ_t = clamp(sum_centered / (sum_sq + ε), -1/α, 1/(1-α))
        //
        // The ε = 1.0 regularizer prevents division by zero and acts
        // as a prior that keeps bets small when evidence is scarce.
        let centered = z - alpha;
        // These fields tracked in EProcessMonitor (omitted from struct
        // above for clarity, but must be added during implementation):
        //   self.ons_sum_centered += centered;
        //   self.ons_sum_sq += centered * centered;
        //   self.ons_count += 1;
        let lambda_raw = self.ons_sum_centered / (self.ons_sum_sq + 1.0);
        let lambda_min = -1.0 / alpha;
        let lambda_max = 1.0 / (1.0 - alpha);
        lambda_raw.clamp(lambda_min, lambda_max)
    }

    /// Check if miscalibration evidence exceeds threshold.
    /// Crucially, this is VALID at any stopping time — no p-hacking.
    fn miscalibrated(&self) -> bool {
        self.e_value >= self.alert_threshold
    }

    /// Identify WHICH subsystem or agent is causing the drift.
    /// Returns (entity, e_value) pairs sorted by evidence strength.
    fn drift_sources(&self) -> Vec<(String, f64)> {
        let mut sources = Vec::new();
        for (sub, e) in &self.per_subsystem {
            if *e >= self.alert_threshold * 0.5 { // early warning
                sources.push((format!("subsystem:{sub:?}"), *e));
            }
        }
        for (agent, e) in &self.per_agent {
            if *e >= self.alert_threshold * 0.5 {
                sources.push((format!("agent:{agent}"), *e));
            }
        }
        sources.sort_by(|a, b| b.1.partial_cmp(&a.1).unwrap_or(std::cmp::Ordering::Equal));
        sources
    }
}
```

### Why This Is Better Than Coverage Counting
- Coverage counting: "I was 78% accurate over the last 50 decisions" → SINGLE number
- E-process: "I have 18.5x evidence of miscalibration, primarily from
  liveness decisions about AgentB (agent-specific e = 42.3), with the shift
  starting ~30 decisions ago" → PINPOINTS the problem + QUANTIFIES evidence

## Core Component 2: CUSUM Regime Change Detector

### Theory
CUSUM (Cumulative Sum) detects shifts in the mean of a sequence by accumulating
deviations from an expected value. When the accumulated sum exceeds a threshold,
a regime change is declared. This is fundamentally different from the e-process:
the e-process detects *overall* miscalibration; CUSUM detects *when* the
behavior changed.

### Implementation
```rust
struct CusumDetector {
    /// Running CUSUM statistic (resets to 0 on regime change detection)
    s_pos: f64,  // detects upward shift (more errors than expected)
    s_neg: f64,  // detects downward shift (fewer errors = improvement)
    /// Expected error rate under null hypothesis
    expected_rate: f64,
    /// Detection threshold (calibrated for false alarm rate)
    threshold: f64,
    /// Minimum shift magnitude to detect
    delta: f64,
    /// When the current regime started
    regime_start: i64,
    /// History of detected regime changes
    regime_changes: VecDeque<RegimeChange>,
}

struct RegimeChange {
    timestamp: i64,
    direction: ChangeDirection, // Degradation | Improvement
    pre_rate: f64,
    post_rate: f64,
    cusum_value: f64,
}

impl CusumDetector {
    fn update(&mut self, error_occurred: bool, timestamp: i64) {
        let x = if error_occurred { 1.0 } else { 0.0 };
        // Page's CUSUM update
        self.s_pos = (self.s_pos + x - self.expected_rate - self.delta / 2.0).max(0.0);
        self.s_neg = (self.s_neg - x + self.expected_rate - self.delta / 2.0).max(0.0);

        if self.s_pos > self.threshold {
            self.declare_regime_change(ChangeDirection::Degradation, timestamp);
        } else if self.s_neg > self.threshold {
            self.declare_regime_change(ChangeDirection::Improvement, timestamp);
        }
    }
}
```

### Integration with ATC
When CUSUM detects a regime change:
- **Degradation**: immediately switch to safe mode + send alert to #atc-status
  with the exact timestamp and metrics showing the shift
- **Improvement**: log it, potentially exit safe mode if e-process also shows
  recovery, post a positive update to #atc-status

## Core Component 3: Counterfactual Regret Tracker

### Theory (from §12.3 OCO + §0.19 Evidence Ledger)
For every decision the ATC makes, compute what WOULD have happened if it had
chosen differently. The regret is the difference between what we did and the
best action in hindsight.

### Implementation
```rust
struct RegretTracker {
    /// Cumulative regret per action type
    cumulative_regret: HashMap<String, f64>,
    /// Recent regret window for trend detection
    recent_regrets: VecDeque<RegretEntry>,
    /// FTRL (Follow the Regularized Leader) for adaptive loss matrix tuning
    loss_matrix_adjuster: LossMatrixFTRL,
}

struct RegretEntry {
    decision_id: u64,
    chosen_action: String,
    chosen_loss: f64,
    best_hindsight_action: String,
    best_hindsight_loss: f64,
    regret: f64, // chosen_loss - best_hindsight_loss
    timestamp: i64,
}

impl RegretTracker {
    /// After observing ground truth, compute regret for a past decision.
    fn record_outcome(&mut self, decision: &AtcDecisionRecord, actual_state: &str) {
        let actual_loss = decision.loss_matrix_entry(&decision.action, actual_state);
        let best_action = decision.best_action_for_state(actual_state);
        let best_loss = decision.loss_matrix_entry(&best_action, actual_state);
        let regret = actual_loss - best_loss;

        self.cumulative_regret
            .entry(decision.action.clone())
            .and_modify(|r| *r += regret)
            .or_insert(regret);

        self.recent_regrets.push_back(RegretEntry {
            decision_id: decision.id,
            chosen_action: decision.action.clone(),
            chosen_loss: actual_loss,
            best_hindsight_action: best_action.clone(),
            best_hindsight_loss: best_loss,
            regret,
            timestamp: decision.timestamp_micros,
        });
    }

    /// Use FTRL to suggest loss matrix adjustments based on observed regret.
    /// If the ATC consistently over-penalizes "suspect" when agents are alive,
    /// FTRL will suggest reducing L(suspect, alive) — making the ATC less
    /// trigger-happy about suspecting agents.
    fn suggest_loss_adjustments(&self) -> Vec<(String, String, f64)> {
        // Returns: [(action, state, suggested_delta)]
        self.loss_matrix_adjuster.compute_gradient()
    }
}
```

### Why Counterfactual Regret Is Powerful
Without it: the ATC has no idea if its loss matrix values are right.
With it: the ATC continuously learns "I keep declaring agents dead when
they're just slow — I should increase L(release_reservations, alive) from
100 to 150 to be more cautious."

This is NOT self-modifying code — the FTRL suggests adjustments that are
logged to the evidence ledger and only applied when the operator approves
(or when running in auto-tune mode: AM_ATC_AUTO_TUNE_LOSS_MATRIX=true).

## Composing the Three Components

```
Event → E-Process (is it miscalibrated?) ─── YES → safe mode
  │                                            │
  │     CUSUM (when did behavior change?) ─── regime change → alert
  │                                            │
  │     Regret Tracker (what should we have done?) → loss matrix tuning
  │                                            │
  └── All three feed into Evidence Ledger ─────┘
```

The composition is non-interfering (they read the same evidence stream but
maintain independent state) and fail-safe (if any component has a bug, the
others still work, and the worst case is the existing safe mode behavior).

## New Configuration Variables
| Variable | Default | Description |
|----------|---------|-------------|
| AM_ATC_EPROCESS_ALERT_THRESHOLD | 20.0 | E-process evidence for miscalibration (1/α) |
| AM_ATC_CUSUM_THRESHOLD | 5.0 | CUSUM detection threshold for regime changes |
| AM_ATC_CUSUM_DELTA | 0.1 | Minimum shift magnitude CUSUM should detect |
| AM_ATC_REGRET_WINDOW | 100 | Recent decisions for regret trend analysis |
| AM_ATC_AUTO_TUNE_LOSS_MATRIX | false | Auto-apply FTRL loss matrix suggestions |

## Dependency
- Requires Track 1 (Decision Core) — operates on decision outputs
- Requires Track 4 (Evidence Ledger) — reads and enriches decision records
- REPLACES the simple CalibrationWindow approach in Track 5
- Track 5 becomes the WRAPPER that decides safe mode based on the signals
  from this track's three components

## Files
- crates/mcp-agent-mail-server/src/atc.rs (martingale module within ATC)

## Tests
- Unit: e-process starts at 1.0, stays below threshold under well-calibrated predictions
- Unit: e-process exceeds threshold after sustained miscalibration
- Unit: per-subsystem e-process identifies the drifting subsystem correctly
- Unit: per-agent e-process identifies the problematic agent
- Unit: CUSUM detects mean shift within O(1/δ²) observations
- Unit: CUSUM does NOT false-alarm under stationary error rates
- Unit: CUSUM distinguishes degradation from improvement
- Unit: regret tracker computes correct counterfactual losses
- Unit: FTRL loss matrix suggestions reduce cumulative regret on replay data
- Unit: composition of all three produces correct safe-mode signals
- Property: e-process is a valid supermartingale (E[E_{t+1}|E_t] <= E_t under H₀)
- Property: CUSUM false alarm rate <= configured budget over 100K simulated ticks
- Integration: all three components feed into evidence ledger correctly
- Performance: full update cycle (e-process + CUSUM + regret) < 10μs

