# Track 12: Predictive Coordination Intelligence

## Graveyard Sources
- §12.2 Causal Inference / do-calculus [Tier B]
- §12.14 Contextual Bandits for Runtime Autotuning [Tier B]
- §10.3 Learning-Augmented Online Algorithms [Tier A]

## What This Adds
A causal model of agent coordination that reasons about WHY conflicts happen
and PREDICTS them before any reservation overlap occurs. The current Track 3
conflict detector is reactive — it sees overlapping reservations and reports
them. This track adds PREDICTIVE intelligence that understands the causal
structure of agent workflows and intervenes preemptively.

## The Causal Insight
Multi-agent conflicts follow a causal DAG:

```
TaskAssignment → FileSelection → ReservationRequest → Overlap → Conflict
                     ↑
              ThreadParticipation
```

By the time we see "Overlap", it's already too late — the agents have both
started working. The ATC should intervene at the "FileSelection" stage, which
is observable through message content (agents discuss which files they plan
to modify).

## Core Idea: Message-Driven Intent Inference

### Thread Participation Graph
Track which agents are participating in which threads. Agents in the SAME
thread working on the SAME feature are the highest conflict risk:

```rust
struct ThreadParticipationGraph {
    /// thread_id → set of participating agents
    thread_agents: HashMap<String, HashSet<String>>,
    /// agent → set of threads they're active in
    agent_threads: HashMap<String, HashSet<String>>,
    /// agent pair → number of shared threads (conflict proximity)
    shared_thread_count: HashMap<(String, String), usize>,
}
```

When `shared_thread_count(A, B) >= 2` AND neither A nor B has reservations,
the ATC sends a proactive advisory: "You and {other} are both active in
threads {list}. Consider coordinating file reservations before editing."

### Information-Theoretic Probe Scheduling
The liveness detector (Track 2) sends probes at a fixed interval. But probes
are expensive (they add messages to the system). We should probe the agents
we're MOST uncertain about — i.e., maximize information gain per probe.

```rust
/// Compute the expected information gain from probing this agent.
/// Based on the entropy of the posterior belief about their liveness.
fn probe_information_gain(posterior: &[(AgentLivenessState, f64)]) -> f64 {
    let entropy = posterior.iter()
        .filter(|(_, p)| *p > 0.0)
        .map(|(_, p)| -p * p.ln())
        .sum::<f64>();

    // High entropy = high uncertainty = high info gain from probing
    // Low entropy = we're already confident = don't waste a probe
    entropy
}

/// Schedule probes in order of expected information gain.
/// Only probe agents where the info gain exceeds a threshold.
fn schedule_probes(agents: &HashMap<String, AgentState>) -> Vec<String> {
    let mut candidates: Vec<(String, f64)> = agents.iter()
        .filter(|(_, state)| state.probe_eligible())
        .map(|(name, state)| {
            let gain = probe_information_gain(&state.posterior());
            (name.clone(), gain)
        })
        .collect();
    candidates.sort_by(|a, b| b.1.partial_cmp(&a.1).unwrap_or(std::cmp::Ordering::Equal));
    // Only probe agents where info gain > threshold (don't waste probes on
    // agents we're already confident about)
    candidates.into_iter()
        .filter(|(_, gain)| *gain > 0.3) // ~bits of information
        .map(|(name, _)| name)
        .collect()
}
```

### Contextual Bandit for Advisory Effectiveness
Different agents respond differently to ATC advisories. Some agents immediately
reserve files when warned; others ignore advisories entirely. The ATC should
LEARN which advisory style works for each agent and adapt.

```rust
enum AdvisoryStyle {
    /// Brief factual: "[ATC] Overlap risk: src/lib.rs"
    Brief,
    /// Detailed with reasoning: "[ATC] Overlap risk: src/lib.rs. BlueFox
    /// also editing this area. Consider reserving to avoid merge conflicts."
    Detailed,
    /// Actionable command: "[ATC] Reserved src/lib.rs for you to prevent
    /// conflict with BlueFox. Release when done."
    Proactive,
}

struct AdvisoryBandit {
    /// Per-agent Thompson sampling posteriors for each style
    /// (alpha, beta) of Beta distribution for success rate
    per_agent: HashMap<String, [(f64, f64); 3]>,
}

impl AdvisoryBandit {
    /// Select the best advisory style for this agent using Thompson sampling.
    fn select_style(&self, agent: &str) -> AdvisoryStyle {
        let params = self.per_agent.get(agent)
            .unwrap_or(&[(1.0, 1.0); 3]); // uniform prior
        // Sample from Beta(alpha, beta) for each style
        // Pick the style with the highest sample
        // This automatically balances exploration vs exploitation
    }

    /// Update after observing whether the agent acted on the advisory.
    fn update(&mut self, agent: &str, style: AdvisoryStyle, acted: bool) {
        let params = self.per_agent.entry(agent.to_string())
            .or_insert([(1.0, 1.0); 3]);
        let idx = style as usize;
        if acted {
            params[idx].0 += 1.0; // alpha += 1
        } else {
            params[idx].1 += 1.0; // beta += 1
        }
    }
}
```

### How "Acted On" Is Determined
- Agent receives advisory about file X
- Within 5 minutes, agent creates a reservation for file X → ACTED
- Within 5 minutes, agent sends message to the other agent → ACTED (coordinated)
- 5 minutes pass with no relevant action → NOT ACTED
- This feedback loop is fully automatic from event observation

